      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Aside from TTL and caching, there are a few other factors that could cause delays in DNS updates:\n\n1. Propagation Time: After updating a DNS record, it takes time for the changes to propagate throughout the global DNS system. This process can take anywhere from a few minutes to 48 hours, depending on the TTL and the efficiency of the DNS servers.\n\n2. DNS Server Configuration: Some DNS servers may have different update intervals or may not be configured to check for updates as frequently as others. This can lead to inconsistencies in which DNS servers show the updated IP address.\n\n3. ISP-Level Caching: Your Internet Service Provider (ISP) may have its own caching mechanism that stores DNS records for a certain period. This can cause delays in seeing the updated IP address on your local machine.\n\n4. Network Congestion: High network traffic or congestion can slow down the propagation of DNS updates, especially if the DNS servers are located far from your location.\n\n5. DNS Server Issues: Sometimes, DNS servers themselves may have issues or be experiencing downtime, which can affect the propagation of DNS updates.\n\nWhile these factors can contribute to delays, waiting for the TTL to expire and clearing your local DNS cache are usually the most effective solutions. If the issue",
